WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.537 --> 00:00:02.000
[David Landsman, PhD]
When I was a child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.125 --> 00:00:04.601
I spent a lot of time thinking on my own.

00:00:04.601 --> 00:00:07.648
There was a huge oak tree in&nbsp;
our backyard and I used to sit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.648 --> 00:00:11.284
and the wind always blew. The&nbsp;
thing was moving all the time.

00:00:11.334 --> 00:00:17.820
And I used to think about all sorts of things&nbsp;
about life and and and how things worked.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.504
I became a trained biochemist and I was&nbsp;
doing a post-doc on the NIH campus. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.504 --> 00:00:30.000
I was sequencing genes. I realized very quickly&nbsp;
that you cannot do that without a computer.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.900
So I started learning how to use a&nbsp;
computer, how to set up computers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.020
how to expand computers to deal&nbsp;
with bigger amounts of data.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.460
And soon became the expert in the&nbsp;
lab on computational biology methods.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:44.880
The research I'm doing now is about turning genes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.340
on and off and in order to do that we&nbsp;
need to analyze very large data sets.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.490
And to analyze large data sets, we use software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:58.680
that we develop to process the&nbsp;
data on significant computers.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:04.620
Our research is more designed around&nbsp;
what the natural process is in the cell.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:10.869
Things in the nucleus are not flat. The&nbsp;
chromosomes fold into chromatin and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:13.380
chromatin itself folds into higher structures.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:18.660
And all these are in some sort&nbsp;
of flux at all times. So when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:25.860
they become used for up-regulation or&nbsp;
down-regulation to create more protein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:33.835
all of that is one big concert of events taking&nbsp;
place to create or suppress a different function.

00:01:33.835 --> 00:01:40.918
Our work on the natural process of biology acts&nbsp;
as a foundation for clinicians, geneticists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.918 --> 00:01:44.700
and physicians to try and understand&nbsp;
what happens in people that get sick.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:51.334
You have to have an imagination. If you don't&nbsp;
have an imagination, it's hard to do science.

00:01:51.334 --> 00:01:52.801
I think I'll always be this curious &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.801 --> 00:01:58.427
and I'll turn it up again when my grandchildren can&nbsp;
be challenged with these questions.

00:01:58.427 --> 00:02:03.060
The beauty of handing the curiousness over&nbsp;
to the next generation is very important.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:08.069
But I'm looking forward to the time&nbsp;
that I can walk around a field and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:12.240
explain to them things that they never&nbsp;
thought of and make them more curious.

00:02:12.554 --> 00:02:13.460
[Music]

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:18.499
[Music fades to silence]

